# =============================================================================
# NEVER COMMIT CREDENTIALS. This project has already had one real incident
# (2026-08-10) where a sandbox password was briefly pushed in a profiles.yml
# to a temporarily-public repo - see
# docs/PROJECT_CONTEXT_ABI_Health_MASTER.md Section 1 for the full story.
# profiles.yml itself is safe to commit (every value is env_var()-driven,
# no literals) - but .env, any *_credentials* file, or any copy of
# profiles.yml with real values hand-typed in for "just testing" is NOT.
# If you ever do this by accident: rotate the password immediately, don't
# just remove the commit - git history keeps it. Rewriting history isn't
# enough either if the repo was ever public even briefly - assume it's
# compromised and rotate.
# =============================================================================

.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*credentials*
*secrets*

# dbt
target/
dbt_packages/
logs/
.user.yml

# dbt's partial-parse cache is a real, proven gotcha in this project - it
# survived a machine restart once and masked a genuinely-fixed file (see
# docs Section 17 Part G). Not committing it also avoids that ever
# happening via git (e.g. someone else's cache from a different schema.yml
# version landing in your checkout).
partial_parse.msgpack

# Airflow
airflow.db
airflow.cfg
airflow-webserver.pid
standalone_admin_password.txt
webserver_config.py

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
venv/
airflow_venv/
*.egg-info/

# Validation script outputs - these contain real production data pulled
# during testing (claim IDs, tenant names, row counts). Not credentials,
# but also not something to casually commit - regenerate them by re-running
# the script instead.
scale_test_*.csv
parity_results*.csv
full_count_*.csv
*_results.csv

# OS/editor noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
